1	// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
2	//! Responses item assembly under the canonical rules (spec § 5, § 6.2, § 6.4, § 15).
3	//!
4	//! The decided value of a client-run item is always its `output_item.done` item (§ 5). The rules
5	//! make every other view a client SDK reads agree with it: the `added` item (the subset rule, A2),
6	//! the deltas and `*.done` events (R11–R14), and the item at the same index in the terminal
7	//! `output` (R9, R10). A stream that breaks a rule is a [`Deviation`], named after § 6.4.
8	//!
9	//! R5 lets one item be open at a time, so [`Assembly`] is one item-at-a-time state machine. Per
10	//! response it keeps the item ids and call ids (R8), the identity fields of every item (R9), and a
11	//! hash of every client-run `done` item (R10).
12	//!
13	//! Deltas are never retained. Each delta stream keeps a running SHA-256 hash, which is compared
14	//! with the hash of the `*.done` text and of the `done` item's text. So only the open item's
15	//! `added` item and the completed calls hold bytes ([`Assembly::retained`]). When the calls hold
16	//! more than the cap, they are dropped and the hold is never decided (749 D13, as an observed
17	//! hold): see [`Assembly::overflowed`]. Every rule is still checked after that point.
18	//!
19	//! The order of the checks on one event is: R1, R7, then the rules of the event's scope. After a
20	//! deviation, every later call to [`Assembly::accept`] returns the same deviation.
21	
